# Level 3's waves and scenery as the original runs them: levels 1 and 2 won
# at their frames 20 and 30 ($A07 and $A6C set), so the game loads level 3
# as it would; the ship kept alive, still and firing, the shops skipped (as
# level-run.exp). Logged every frame ($3E42) as level2-objs.exp, every token
# taken ($4FF8), and every update of the level's own objects as it starts
# (the snake $4F244, $4F24E, $4F258, the edge enemy $4F2D8, the turrets
# $504B0, the lizards $50690, $507B2, $5073C, the walkers $50818 and their
# bolts $50996, the small walls $50FF6, the big walls $50A6A, $50CF8): its
# pc, object, type, x, y, +40, +48, hit points, +42, +44 and +62. For
# porting level 3's own code frame by frame.
# Run with --trace fdc.
expect prompt read sector sector=0x1 .*tr=0x0 .*addr=0x6760e
stage level2-jump.ini
stage level3-a-start.ini
stage level2-frame.ini
stage weapons-take.ini
stage waves-kill.ini
stage level3-obj.ini
stage level-run-shield.ini
stage level-run-nodeath.ini
stage level-run-nowall.ini
stage level-run-noshop.ini
# Hatari 2.6 loses its breakpoints when a hit adds the 16th (it grows the
# array without copying it): grow it here, outside any hit, then clear it.
debug b pc = $1
debug b pc = $2
debug b pc = $3
debug b pc = $4
debug b pc = $5
debug b pc = $6
debug b pc = $7
debug b pc = $8
debug b pc = $9
debug b pc = $a
debug b pc = $b
debug b pc = $c
debug b pc = $d
debug b pc = $e
debug b pc = $f
debug b pc = $10
debug b pc = $11
debug b pc = $12
debug b pc = $13
debug b pc = $14
debug b pc = $15
debug b pc = $16
debug b all
debug b pc = $7a50 :once :trace :file {run}/level3-a-start.ini
disk-a {disk2}
sleep 8
fire
sleep 4
fire
expect level1-read read sector sector=0x3 .*tr=0xc .*addr=0x3e
quiet level1-loaded 3
fire
sleep 2
fire
sleep 2
